A||N||5||fastfiero||A/C compresser cluch wont spin||||1||
Z||000000||fastfiero||08-23-2000||06:22 PM||||I live in texas and right know it is very hot i need my A/C. It will blow warm air when the fan speed is on low and mid bit NOT hi. Also the cluch on the compressor will not engage. And the engine cooling fan always runs. Its an 87 GT. Can any one help me?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||08-24-2000||09:19 AM||||If you are low on freon the pressure switch will not let the compressor engage.  <P>You can check the pressure with a tire-pressure like gauge that you can get from AutoZone or other parts stores.  If it is low you will need to find the leak and fix it before you can have reliable A/C again.<P>Interdynamics sells a R-134 conversion kit that does not require opening the system.  You have to pull vacuum on the system to remove the R-12.  You then add two cans of R-134 and an R-134 oil charge (supposed to be compatible with the existing oil). <P>The kit cost $32.00 at AutoZone. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||08-24-2000||09:54 AM||||If you have a leak, you might as well find out why before you dump R134 into your system.<P>And you might as well do it right or you'll be looking at the same problem again at some point down the road.<P>Check out Ogre's articles on his site re: retroing A/C. Pretty much spells everything out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||08-24-2000||11:13 AM||||i did the Autozone R134 conversionless conversion on my GT 2 weeks ago... it lasted 1 day b4 the compressor seized!  my system had been disoconected for some time.<P>i now believe what they say about replacing the acumulator b4 doing this.<P>as for the clutch, if that what keeps it from spinning, that has to be cheaper than replacing the whole compressor... i would like to know more about that, myself.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||capt_ron||08-24-2000||12:21 PM||||Steer clear of the quickie change over to R134 unless you want to replace the compressor. The oils are not compatable and the compressor will lock up. As for the clutch not engaging, check the low side pressure switch. It's on the accumilator in the front compartment. Pull the plug off and jumper the connections, if the compressor comes on then you either have a bad switch or need a top off charge. If the pressure is up to snuff then the switch can be replaced without opening the system. My 87 SE quit working and I was unable to find the reason that the clutch wouldn't engage. I swapped around the relays and tried everything but no luck. One day I asked my GF if she had tried it while going down the road, she called me from work and said that it worked great and has since, go figure.<BR>One last point, ALL automotive systems will lose charge over the years. The compressor seals will leak and that's why it's recommended to cycle the A/C even during the winter months. There is usually nothing wrong with the integrity of the system if after several years it needs a top off charge. The EPA recognizes this and allows the addition of R12 to put the system back in service. Most shops however seize the chance to make a buck off you.  <BR>Good luck.<BR>Capt Ron<p>[This message has been edited by capt_ron (edited 08-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||ourufo||08-24-2000||09:10 PM||||COOL.. To change over from r12 to r134a you must spend money, LOTS!  r134a has a smaller moleculer structor so it WILL find small leaks in old hoses much easer. On top of that , r134a runs slightly higher pressure than r12. therefor find ing leaks faster.<BR>If you change it, change it all.<BR>Use leak dye it's much more cost efctive in the long run.<BR><P>------------------<BR>OURUFOV8||||reg||6
